WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.070
- Our next guest spent five seasons

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.460
as Jane's hotel owning baby
daddy and sometimes boyfriend

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.250
on the very popular
telenovela, "Jane the Virgin".

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.040
He's out now with a new book.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.210
it's called "Man Enough
Undefining my Masculinity".

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:14.972
Please welcome Justin Baldoni.

00:00:14.972 --> 00:00:15.932
(audience cheering)

00:00:15.932 --> 00:00:18.932
(upbeat jazz music)

00:00:21.917 --> 00:00:22.750
- Hi.

00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:27.226
- I wish I could hug you.

00:00:27.226 --> 00:00:28.059
- Aw.

00:00:28.059 --> 00:00:29.020
- May I lead with the thing

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.290
that everyone leads with,
you are very talented

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.990
but your hair is just unbelievable.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.270
Your unbelievable hair.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:36.103
- Kelly Clarkson just gave me a.

00:00:36.103 --> 00:00:37.260
- [Kelly] Have you done
a hair commercial yet?

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.593
This is the question I have.

00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:39.426
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:39.426 --> 00:00:40.259
- Oh I've been, I've been

00:00:40.259 --> 00:00:41.940
in at home by myself,
you know, with my family.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:42.773
- [Kelly] Well.

00:00:42.773 --> 00:00:43.810
- So this is exciting.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:44.920
- [Kelly] Should've
made a hair commercial.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:45.775
- Well, thank you.

00:00:45.775 --> 00:00:46.608
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:46.608 --> 00:00:47.566
Your hair is amazing.

00:00:47.566 --> 00:00:48.399
- This is half mine.

00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:49.850
- Also blue, I've tried to.
- We called each other.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.560
I was like, you wear your
acid wash colors, but you are

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.400
you are doing well as
an actor and director.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.560
So why write a book?

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:57.610
Did you always wanna write a book?

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.737
- No actually, I tried to get
out of this book a few times.

00:01:00.737 --> 00:01:02.571
(audience and Kelly laughs)

00:01:02.571 --> 00:01:05.850
And the irony is I wrote a
book called Man Enough Yet.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.800
I think don't feel man
enough to have written it.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.515
And for me, the book is
it's undefining masculinity.

00:01:12.515 --> 00:01:15.350
It's taking men out

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.340
of this constricting box that
doesn't just hurt the women

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.220
and gender nonconforming,
and trans people.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.740
But also, we hurt ourselves

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.310
and I didn't realize how
much I was suffering.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.010
And so many men I know
are suffering, but we just

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:29.870
we can't ask for help.

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:30.703
We can't talk about it.

00:01:30.703 --> 00:01:31.536
Cause we talk about it.

00:01:31.536 --> 00:01:33.943
We're challenging the system
that keeps us in the box.

00:01:33.943 --> 00:01:34.776
- Yeah.

00:01:34.776 --> 00:01:36.940
- And we're hurting ourselves.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:38.690
We're walking around miserable and unhappy

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:40.220
even though we're putting
on a face that we are.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:44.305
So a book was, it was kind of
like a 2.0 of the Ted talk.

00:01:44.305 --> 00:01:46.610
It was a way to put everything

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.820
on the page and it was
also really healing for me.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.870
And it came out because I really believe

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:52.540
that the only way that we can heal is

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:53.810
if we can share our stories

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:55.110
so that other people can find their own.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:55.943
And a lot of us men

00:01:55.943 --> 00:01:58.780
are not comfortable
talking about these things.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.870
- And it's a shame
because you're missing out

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.733
on all those extra layers of
the onion with men as well.

00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:05.566
- Yeah.

00:02:05.566 --> 00:02:06.400
- And they're missing out on it.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.130
I heard your wife had a suggestion.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.260
This is amazing.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:10.635
I heard your wife had a suggestion

00:02:10.635 --> 00:02:11.468
- I think I know where you're going.

00:02:11.468 --> 00:02:12.720
- after finishing the book.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:13.553
she read the book.

00:02:13.553 --> 00:02:15.090
And what was the suggestion?

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.017
Tell everyone.

00:02:16.017 --> 00:02:18.070
- It was the first, it was the end.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.810
It was the, it was the last chapter.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:21.657
And she looks at me and she says,

00:02:21.657 --> 00:02:24.423
"Baby, this is really, really good.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.530
But I think you need
to read your own book."

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.479
- Oh my God, that's a real talk.

00:02:29.479 --> 00:02:30.340
(Kelly and audience chuckles)

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.580
- And the thing is, is it's true.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.890
And I wrote this book, not as an expert

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.140
but as somebody who is in it,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:37.020
I'm in the fire.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.170
- I'm under construction.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:40.003
- That's the point.

00:02:40.003 --> 00:02:42.950
And, and she reminded me that I'm like

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:45.580
I need to go back and
reread a lot of the chapters

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.190
especially the chapters where
I'm reminding other people

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.760
that they are enough.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.620
You are enough because I struggle

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.540
with my enoughness every single day.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:54.373
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- But I put

00:02:54.373 --> 00:02:56.270
on my armor and I'm not
allowed to express it.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:57.103
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:57.103 --> 00:02:58.447
But boy is it liberating
to be able to say like

00:02:58.447 --> 00:03:00.980
"Oh my God, I'm a nervous being
on the Kelly Clarkson show,

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:01.970
this is awesome."

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.100
Because it makes us human

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.250
and we can have a real connection.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.200
- Yeah, and what you're
talking about is it's not

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.033
only progressing you,

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:09.020
but it's progressing
society's way of thinking.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.730
And then that's progressing
the whole world.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.560
I think that's a really great
conversation to be done.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:13.970
- But these aren't new ideas, as you know

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:14.993
- No, I know, but none of the.

00:03:14.993 --> 00:03:16.070
- These are super old ideas and honestly,

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:17.470
women have been talking
about this stuff forever.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:20.150
I'm just like, I'm just a
straight white privileged dude.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:20.983
That's talking about it.

00:03:20.983 --> 00:03:23.290
- I know but like, feel like
it's that thing, like when you

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:24.820
if you haven't gone to
church, I grew up in church

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.010
like growing up in church and it was like

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.510
every Sunday I was like,
is he talking to me?

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:29.980
Like the pastor, like the message.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.089
I was like, wait, or if I hadn't been

00:03:31.089 --> 00:03:33.028
in a while and I'd come
and I'd be like, Oh.

00:03:33.028 --> 00:03:33.861
- Camera zooms in just on you.

00:03:33.861 --> 00:03:34.770
- I'm like, I'm like, I'm like,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:35.740
he knows I haven't been here.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:36.573
This is about me.

00:03:36.573 --> 00:03:38.320
Like I'm like, and so
I start to freak out.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.520
But I think it's that
logical thing that would be

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.900
reminded things that you do know innately

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.266
but we just don't practice on
the daily because we get lost

00:03:45.266 --> 00:03:47.716
and how busy we are and,
Oh, this is how it goes.

00:03:47.716 --> 00:03:48.549
It's okay.

00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:49.390
This is how I was taught.
- Yeah.

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:50.710
- And you don't, we don't progress.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:51.760
I think it's really important.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:52.850
I think this is awesome.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:53.683
- Oh.

00:03:53.683 --> 00:03:55.207
- So everybody, Justin's book is called

00:03:55.207 --> 00:03:58.160
"Man Enough Undefining my
Masculinity" it's out now.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.537
You gotta check it out.

